# 2D Rigid Body Physics Engine & Sandbox (Python)

A modular, from-scratch 2D physics simulation environment built entirely in native Python. This project was developed as a deep-dive exploration of linear algebra, explicit numerical integration methods, and rigid body kinetics. It features a completely decoupled architecture separating core physics computation, UI rendering, and front-end application logic.

## Technical Highlights
* **Native Linear Algebra Package:** Built a custom 2D vector mathematics library (`Math2D`) complete with unit tests to manage translation, scale, dot products, and coordinate transformation sets.
* **Rigid Body Dynamics Engine:** Implements rigid body kinematics (`RigidBody2D`), velocity/acceleration integration profiles, mass properties, and force accumulation loops.
* **Decoupled Visual Stack:** Uses an isolated rendering module (`UI/Renderer.py`) to keep the visual drawing logic completely separated from the internal state changes of the physics engine.
* **Engine Demonstration Sandboxes:** Validates engine performance across multiple runtime targets, including an explicit structural physics suite (`UITest`), a pool/billiards simulator (`Pool.py`), and a table tennis application (`TT.py`).

## Architecture & Directory Structure
```
├── Math2D/                 # Low-level vector math framework
│   └── Vec2D.py            # Native 2D vector class and geometric methods
├── Physics2D/              # Rigid body kinematics core
│   ├── Core.py             # Global simulation tick manager & state engine
│   ├── RigidBody2D.py      # Mass, velocity, and force integration models
│   └── UITest.py           # Explicit physics sandbox testbed
├── UI/                     # Presentation layer
│   └── Renderer.py         # Standalone rendering framework for screen space
└── Games/                  # Sandbox applications leveraging the engine
├── Pool.py             # Billiards/collision mechanics demonstration
├── Crafty.py             # Billiards/collision mechanics demonstration
└── TT.py               # Table Tennis kinematics application
```
